    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Did you have trouble sitting still like this when you were a child?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, yeah. Definitely. I remember getting in trouble all the time back in grade school for just... popping right out of my seat. I had this teacher, Mrs. Gable—she wore those big glasses with the beaded chain on them so she wouldn't lose them, you know? But she was always leaving them on the chalkboard ledge anyway. They used real chalk back then, not those dry erase markers that smell like rubbing alcohol and dry out if somebody leaves the cap off for two seconds. We go through boxes of those markers at the college, it's a huge waste of the supply budget. \n\nBut, um... right, in class, I’d be jumping up, or I’d be talking right over her, or I'd just rush ahead on the worksheet before she even finished giving the directions. My mother used to say I acted like I was wound too tight, even as a little kid. Like one of those little tin wind-up toys? I actually had this tin monkey that played the cymbals, and you'd wind the key in its back and it would just go crazy. My brother hated that thing. He actually threw it in the creek behind our house once. We lived right near Willow Creek, which was great for catching crawdads, but man, the mosquitoes in July...",
